WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:02.340
Hey, this is Presh Talwalkar.

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:06.320
Here's a math problem that's stumping the Internet right now.

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.600
This question was asked to 5th graders in China:

00:00:10.420 --> 00:00:16.170
''If a ship had 26 sheep and 10 goats aboard, how old is the ship's captain?''

00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:23.129
This problem has gotten a lot of media coverage, and I even covered it in a previous video,

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:26.699
But we all missed the real story.

00:00:27.670 --> 00:00:33.569
This problem was actually devised by French researchers nearly 40 years ago!

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:37.770
And it does teach a valuable lesson about math class.

00:00:38.739 --> 00:00:44.459
So in this video, I'm gonna go over the REAL story, that no media outlet has covered so far.

00:00:45.969 --> 00:00:51.989
Before I get to that, I want to quickly go over the media coverage, and what some possible answers have been proposed.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:58.859
So this problem really got coverage around the world. From the South China Morning Post,

00:00:59.860 --> 00:01:01.280
from RT,

00:01:02.040 --> 00:01:04.040
from the BBC,

00:01:04.869 --> 00:01:07.919
from the Washington Post and even in Newsweek.

00:01:10.020 --> 00:01:13.580
The media coverage focused on solving the problem.

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:22.980
The most sensible answer I saw, that was quoted in the BBC, comes from one Weiho commenter who writes:

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.500
''The total weight of 26 sheep and 10 goat

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:31.240
is 7,700 kilograms, based on the average weight of each animal.''

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:39.040
Now I did a little bit of fact-checking; you would actually get 7,700 POUNDS, if you did this search in Wolfram Alpha,

00:01:39.549 --> 00:01:46.529
but, let's just forget this little nitpick and go with 7,700 kilograms, so continuing that response:

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.220
''In China, if you're driving a ship that has more than

00:01:50.520 --> 00:01:55.540
5,000 kilograms of cargo, you need to have possessed a boat license for at least five years.

00:01:56.220 --> 00:02:03.060
The minimum age for getting a boat's license is 23, so [the captain is] at least 28.''

00:02:04.620 --> 00:02:06.620
That's a remarkable leap in logic.

00:02:07.329 --> 00:02:10.378
But if it sounds too crazy for you, keep in mind

00:02:11.050 --> 00:02:15.609
that these kinds of deductions are very common in Sherlock Holmes stories.

00:02:17.330 --> 00:02:20.260
Take an example from ''The Hound of the Baskervilles''.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:25.719
From examining a walking stick of a man he has NEVER met,

00:02:26.390 --> 00:02:32.860
Sherlock Holmes deduces the man is under 30 years old, is a family doctor and that he owns a dog.

00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:43.389
So, the official response in China, is that the question was not a mistake. It was actually meant to be open ended and encourage critical

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:46.510
thinking, which is usually not present in Math class.

00:02:48.040 --> 00:02:53.920
That would be the end of the story, except this problem did NOT have its source in China.

00:02:56.020 --> 00:03:02.800
The real story is the problem was originally asked by French researchers in 1979.

00:03:03.860 --> 00:03:06.540
This problem is nearly 40 years old!

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:15.320
I learned about the problem's origin from Benjamin Dickman, who has a PhD in Mathematics Education from Columbia University.

00:03:16.520 --> 00:03:21.040
Benjamin heard about the problem, because he believes that many people in math education

00:03:21.400 --> 00:03:25.500
have heard about this problem in similar problems, and he tweeted about it.

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:27.640
You could check out his Twitter profile

00:03:28.160 --> 00:03:30.340
twitter.com/benjamindickman

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:35.110
So from the sources that he tweeted and sent,

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:37.280
we can actually find,

00:03:37.280 --> 00:03:42.980
in 1979, French researchers asked first and second graders the following problem:

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:49.320
"On the boat, there are 26 sheep and 10 goats. What is the age of the captain?"

00:03:50.780 --> 00:03:52.780
I couldn't believe it when I was seeing this!

00:03:53.270 --> 00:04:00.339
This problem has been asked nearly 40 YEARS AGO in ALMOST EXACTLY the same wording!

00:04:02.810 --> 00:04:04.810
So what was the point of this exercise?

00:04:07.250 --> 00:04:09.250
Well, from reading some of these sources,

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:15.279
the researchers had expected that most students would think the question is absurd.

00:04:15.680 --> 00:04:19.480
The correct answer would have to be "there is not enough information",

00:04:20.690 --> 00:04:22.690
But what they found was shocking.

00:04:22.790 --> 00:04:28.209
More than 3/4 of students actually tried to solve the problem by manipulating the numbers.

00:04:29.570 --> 00:04:35.230
They said something like ''Okay, the age must be 26 plus 10, which equals 36."

00:04:36.260 --> 00:04:40.140
Very few students questioned if the problem was even solvable.

00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:44.680
In fact, this was not just a fluke result.

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:50.709
The study was replicated on a larger scale in countries including France, Germany and Switzerland,

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:54.820
even by researchers, who thought this was not even possible.

00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:00.440
They were antagonistic to this hypothesis that students would just manipulate numbers, and they found the same thing.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:06.700
Gene Wirchenko emailed me about this related problem.

00:05:08.300 --> 00:05:12.760
His blog has math and logic problems, usually simpler than mine, and it has other posts.

00:05:13.240 --> 00:05:16.080
You can visit his blog at genew.ca

00:05:18.420 --> 00:05:20.700
So, the related question is the following:

00:05:21.460 --> 00:05:24.480
"There are 125 sheep and five dogs in a flock.

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:26.720
How old is the shepherd?"

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:35.720
Well once again, you would expect the ''correct'' answer, that "there is not enough information" to solve the problem.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:40.060
But, researchers found shocking results again!

00:05:40.640 --> 00:05:44.140
Many students produced answers by manipulating the numbers in the question.

00:05:46.080 --> 00:05:48.340
One student reasoned along the following lines:

00:05:48.980 --> 00:05:53.640
"Well, 125 plus 5 equals 130. That's too old of an age

00:05:54.200 --> 00:05:58.400
and 125 minus 5 equals 120, that's also too old of an age.

00:05:59.060 --> 00:06:01.640
But 125 divided by 5 equals 25

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:02.900
sounds about right.''

00:06:03.860 --> 00:06:06.820
So, the student concluded the shepherd is 25 years old.

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:12.920
So, what's the lesson in all of this?

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:23.040
The so-called ''Chinese'' test question, that went viral was REALLY a French research question from nearly 40 years ago.

00:06:25.160 --> 00:06:29.600
For such an absurd question, the correct answer is that ''there is not enough information''

00:06:31.800 --> 00:06:34.320
I give credit to the school in China for asking this question.

00:06:34.720 --> 00:06:38.340
We still need to improve the critical thinking skills of students in math class.

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:42.500
We still keep thinking that every problem we get is solvable,

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:45.580
and it should be solvable just by manipulating the numbers we get

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:51.980
We should always think ''Does the question actually make sense?'' and '' Is there enough information to solve it?''

00:06:52.780 --> 00:06:57.160
So that's the real story to this viral math question from China.

